{"about":"9e57c04e6bea5242dba708350be852428e2dfdd82675f00e73501f9aa1cd471f","body":"The argument posits a false dichotomy: either one accepts induction to claim progressive description of reality, or one treats science as a self-consistent game with no external reference. Popper's solution is precisely that we can act rationally on the best-tested theory (a pragmatic decision) because it is the most likely to be true (an epistemic hope grounded in critical discussion, not induction), without claiming that the tests logically prove its truth. The conjecture collapses this nuanced distinction, incorrectly concluding that a non-inductive preference must necessarily be devoid of any cognitive significance regarding the world.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"Confusion of pragmatic action with epistemic emptiness"}